<p class="lead-p">The best landscape lighting ideas for Texas homes start with what you already have: a limestone facade, a sprawling live oak, a pool you use until October and an outdoor kitchen that earns its keep in every season. Good lighting does not add clutter. It picks out the strongest features, connects them with safe, soft light and keeps glare out of everyone's eyes.</p>

<p>Below are 25 ideas our designers at Landscape Lighting Texas return to again and again, grouped by part of the property. Each one comes with a practical fixture tip, so you can tell a good plan from a pile of stake lights. Mix and match them; most beautiful systems use six to ten of these ideas, not all twenty-five.</p>

<?= figure('landscape-lighting-texas-home', 'Texas home at night with warm uplighting on the facade, lit trees and a glowing walkway', 'A layered plan: facade, trees and path lights working together in warm 2700K–3000K light.') ?>

<h2>Front of house</h2>

<p>The front elevation is what neighbors, guests and buyers see first. Aim for balance: light the architecture, not the windows, and let the darkness between features do some of the work.</p>

<h3>1. Graze a limestone or Austin stone facade</h3>
<p>Textured stone is made for grazing. Place uplights 6–12 inches from the wall and aim almost straight up so the light skims across the surface and every ledge throws a small shadow. The result reads as depth and craftsmanship instead of a flat, washed wall. <strong>Fixture tip:</strong> use a narrow 15–25° beam in 2700K brass bullets, roughly 4–6 feet apart, and keep them out of window openings. See our <a href="/services/architectural-uplighting/">architectural uplighting</a> service for more examples.</p>

<h3>2. Wash smooth stucco and painted brick evenly</h3>
<p>Smooth surfaces show every hot spot, so the approach flips: pull the fixture back 2–3 feet from the wall and use a wider beam to blend the light. Overlapping wash fixtures give a calm, even glow on Mediterranean and modern farmhouse homes. <strong>Fixture tip:</strong> 40–60° flood optics at 3000K on white or cream stucco; check the overlap at dusk and adjust before the stakes are set.</p>

<h3>3. Frame the front entry with columns</h3>
<p>Columns and pilasters flanking a front door create a natural frame. Light each one from its base with a tight beam so the column glows while the door stays the focal point between them. <strong>Fixture tip:</strong> one narrow-beam (10–15°) uplight per column, placed close and slightly in front; add a glare cowl so the lamp is invisible from the sidewalk.</p>

<h3>4. Light the gables and rooflines sparingly</h3>
<p>A tall gable or a stone chimney can carry a home's silhouette at night, but lighting every peak looks busy. Pick the one or two strongest vertical elements and let the rest fall into soft shadow. <strong>Fixture tip:</strong> a higher-output 8–12 watt LED with a spot beam reaches a second-story gable without needing a floodlight on the eave.</p>

<h3>5. Make the house number easy to find</h3>
<p>A lit address is a small detail that helps guests, delivery drivers and emergency responders. It also makes a strong first impression from the curb. <strong>Fixture tip:</strong> a small wall-wash or adjustable spot aimed across a stone or monument address marker, at the same color temperature as the rest of the facade.</p>

<h2>Trees and gardens</h2>

<p>Texas trees are the backbone of most night scenes. Live oaks, cedar elms, crape myrtles and palms each want a different technique. Our <a href="/services/garden-tree-lighting/">garden and tree lighting</a> page covers the full range.</p>

<?= figure('oak-tree-uplighting', 'Large live oak tree uplit at night with warm light reaching into the canopy', 'Two to four uplights from different angles give a live oak depth instead of a flat, single-sided look.') ?>

<h3>6. Uplight a live oak from several angles</h3>
<p>A mature live oak spreads wider than it is tall, so one light rarely does it justice. Place two to four fixtures around the trunk, some aimed into the main limbs and some reaching toward the outer canopy, so the tree reads as three-dimensional from the street. <strong>Fixture tip:</strong> mix 25° and 36° beams at 2700K. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-light-live-oak-trees/">how to light live oak trees</a> walks through the counts by canopy width.</p>

<h3>7. Moonlight from high in the canopy</h3>
<p>Mounting downlights 20–30 feet up in a big oak casts dappled, leaf-shadow patterns across the lawn, like a full moon through branches. It is the most natural-looking effect in landscape lighting. <strong>Fixture tip:</strong> use adjustable tree-mount downlights with a stainless strap or lag screw, leave slack for growth and run the wire in a UV-rated conduit up the trunk.</p>

<h3>8. Silhouette a crape myrtle against a wall</h3>
<p>Crape myrtles have sculptural, smooth trunks. Place a wide wash behind the tree, aimed at the wall or fence beyond it, and the branches appear as a dark outline against glowing stone. <strong>Fixture tip:</strong> a 60° wash fixture between the plant and the wall, hidden low in the bed; 2700K keeps the backdrop warm.</p>

<h3>9. Shadow-play on a blank wall</h3>
<p>Reverse the idea: light the plant from the front and let it cast a large, soft shadow onto a fence, a garage wall or a side elevation. Agaves, sotols and yuccas throw dramatic shapes. <strong>Fixture tip:</strong> place a medium-beam spot low and a few feet in front of the plant; moving it closer makes the shadow bigger and softer.</p>

<h3>10. Light palms on the coast and in the Valley</h3>
<p>Palms are vertical, so they need light that climbs. A tight beam at the base of each trunk shows the bark texture and catches the underside of the fronds. Along the Gulf Coast, salt air demands solid brass or copper fixtures. <strong>Fixture tip:</strong> one 10–15° uplight per trunk for tall palms, or a well light set on the side the home faces.</p>

<h3>11. Add depth with a few lit planting beds</h3>
<p>Beds of salvia, lantana or ornamental grasses look flat in the dark until a few low fixtures bring them forward. You only need a handful to create a middle ground between the house and the lawn. <strong>Fixture tip:</strong> small area lights or hat-style path lights tucked into the bed at 2700K, 6–10 feet apart.</p>

<h2>Paths and driveways</h2>

<p>Walkway and driveway lighting is about safe footing first and beauty second. Pools of light work better than a lit runway. See our <a href="/services/pathway-driveway-lighting/">pathway and driveway lighting</a> service for layouts.</p>

<?= figure('garden-pathway-lighting', 'Curving garden path at night lit by low path lights with soft overlapping pools of light', 'Staggered path lights create soft pools of light instead of a straight, airport-style line.') ?>

<h3>12. Stagger path lights instead of lining them up</h3>
<p>Two parallel rows of lights on either side of a walk is the most common mistake we see. Alternate sides and space fixtures so the pools of light just touch on the ground. <strong>Fixture tip:</strong> 2–3 watt LED path lights with a hood, spaced 8–10 feet apart and set 6–12 inches back from the walk edge so mowers and feet miss them.</p>

<h3>13. Light steps with recessed step lights</h3>
<p>Stone steps and changes in grade are where people trip. Small fixtures built into the riser cast light down across each tread without any glare. <strong>Fixture tip:</strong> louvered step lights installed every second or third riser during construction, or surface-mount brick lights on existing walls.</p>

<h3>14. Line a long driveway with low bollards</h3>
<p>Country properties and Hill Country lots often have long drives with no streetlights. Low bollards or path lights at the curves mark the edge and make the approach feel welcoming. <strong>Fixture tip:</strong> 24-inch bollards with a downward louver at 30–40 foot intervals, closer at curves and the entry gate.</p>

<h3>15. Mark the driveway edge with in-grade lights</h3>
<p>Flush, drive-over fixtures set into the pavement or the border mark the edge without anything to clip. They suit modern homes and tight drives. <strong>Fixture tip:</strong> choose fixtures rated for vehicle loads with a lens that sheds water; in clay soil, set them on a gravel base so they drain.</p>

<h3>16. Light the entry gate and pillars</h3>
<p>Stone or brick pillars at the drive entrance announce the property after dark. A small uplight on each pillar, or a cap light on top, is enough. <strong>Fixture tip:</strong> match the beam and output on both pillars; even slight differences look lopsided from the road.</p>

<h2>Pools and water</h2>

<p>Water doubles every light near it as a reflection. A few well-placed fixtures around the pool, fountain or pond carry the whole backyard. More ideas on our <a href="/services/pool-water-feature-lighting/">pool and water feature lighting</a> page.</p>

<?= figure('pool-lighting', 'Backyard swimming pool at night with glowing water, lit palms and warm patio light', 'Landscape lighting around the pool frames the water and reflects in it.') ?>

<h3>17. Light the landscape around the pool</h3>
<p>The pool light handles the water, but the plants, walls and trees around it set the mood. Uplight a few palms or a back wall so the reflections dance across the surface. <strong>Fixture tip:</strong> keep fixtures clear of splash zones and wet decks; use 2700K–3000K so the warm surround contrasts with the cooler pool water.</p>

<h3>18. Highlight a fountain or sheer descent</h3>
<p>Moving water catches light beautifully. Small submersible fixtures beneath a spillway or fountain jets make the water itself glow. <strong>Fixture tip:</strong> submersible fixtures with an IP68 rating, cleaned at least twice a year in hard Texas water to remove mineral scale.</p>

<h3>19. Use a reflecting pond or still water as a mirror</h3>
<p>A still pond or rill can reflect a lit tree or facade behind it. Light the object, not the water, and the reflection does the rest. <strong>Fixture tip:</strong> position the uplights on the far side of the water so the beam never shines directly toward the main viewing area.</p>

<h2>Patios and outdoor living</h2>

<p>Texans spend nine or ten months a year outside. Patio lighting should be layered so you can cook, eat and relax under the same fixtures. Our <a href="/services/patio-outdoor-living-lighting/">patio and outdoor living lighting</a> service goes deeper.</p>

<?= figure('patio-lighting', 'Covered patio at night with warm string lights, downlights and a lit outdoor seating area', 'Layered patio light: task light for the kitchen, soft ambient light for seating.') ?>

<h3>20. Downlight pergolas and cabanas</h3>
<p>Small downlights tucked into pergola beams create soft, even light over a dining table or seating area. They are hidden during the day and far less glaring than a single bright floodlight. <strong>Fixture tip:</strong> 3–4 watt adjustable downlights mounted on beams, on a dimmable zone so you can turn them down after dinner.</p>

<h3>21. Task-light the outdoor kitchen</h3>
<p>Grilling in the dark is hard. Under-counter strip lights and a focused downlight over the cooking area give real working light, while the rest of the patio stays warm and dim. <strong>Fixture tip:</strong> 3000K for the kitchen zone, which is a touch crisper for food prep; keep strips sealed and rated for wet locations.</p>

<h3>22. Hang bistro string lights the right way</h3>
<p>String lights sell the Texas backyard feel, but sagging cords and bare cheap bulbs quickly look worn. Use heavy-duty commercial strands on a steel guide wire and tie them to posts or the eave. <strong>Fixture tip:</strong> shatterproof LED filament bulbs at 2200K–2700K, on their own dimmable circuit separate from the landscape lights.</p>

<h3>23. Add steps and seat-wall lights</h3>
<p>Built-in seat walls, fire pits and raised beds are perfect hosts for small hardscape lights. They provide wayfinding and a glow at knee height, which feels intimate. <strong>Fixture tip:</strong> low-profile hardscape lights under the cap stone, installed during construction so the wire is hidden in the wall.</p>

<?= callout('<p>Light the patio in layers on separate zones: one for task lighting, one for ambient downlights and one for the surrounding landscape. Being able to dim each layer is what makes a space feel comfortable at 10pm rather than like a stadium.</p>', 'tip') ?>

<h2>Smart controls and finishing touches</h2>

<p>The last few ideas are about control and quality. They are what make a system easy to live with for years. See our <a href="/services/smart-lighting-systems/">smart lighting systems</a> page for the options.</p>

<h3>24. Run on an astronomical timer with zones</h3>
<p>Sunset in Texas shifts by more than two hours between December and June. An astronomical timer follows dusk automatically, and zones let you leave the facade on all night while the patio switches off at 11pm. <strong>Fixture tip:</strong> pair a multi-tap transformer with a smart controller or app-based module so you can change schedules from your phone. Our <a href="/tools/dusk-timer/">Texas Dusk Timer</a> shows sunset times by month for your city.</p>

<h3>25. Keep color temperature consistent and the light dark-sky friendly</h3>
<p>Nothing breaks a design faster than a mix of warm and cool fixtures. Choose one main color temperature for the whole property and shield every fixture so the light lands on the target, not in the sky or the neighbor's bedroom. <strong>Fixture tip:</strong> 2700K for most stone, plants and trees; 3000K for white stucco or modern homes. Read our <a href="/landscape-lighting-color-temperature-guide/">color temperature guide</a> for details.</p>

