<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

<h3>How many landscape lights does a typical Texas home need?</h3>
<p>A typical front yard uses 10–20 fixtures; a front and back design often lands between 20 and 40. The count depends on the facade width, the number of trees and the length of your paths more than on square footage.</p>

<h3>What is the best color temperature for landscape lighting in Texas?</h3>
<p>2700K suits most limestone, brick, trees and plants because it brings out warm tones. 3000K works well for white stucco, modern homes and outdoor kitchens. Keep one main color temperature across the property for a consistent look.</p>

<h3>Will landscape lighting hold up to Texas heat and hail?</h3>
<p>Professional-grade solid brass or copper fixtures with sealed LED modules handle heat, humidity and coastal salt far better than aluminum or plastic kits. Fixtures sit low, so hail rarely damages them, but lenses should be cleaned and checked each spring.</p>

<h3>Do I need HOA approval for landscape lighting?</h3>
<p>Many Texas HOAs have rules about light trespass, color and brightness, especially for floodlights. Low-glare, shielded, warm white fixtures usually comply easily, but check your deed restrictions before installation.</p>

